-**Scaling:**
-*   Mini-Splatting scales well to larger scenes by maintaining a constant number of Gaussians, reducing memory usage and increasing rendering speed [S2].
+*   **Model Size Reduction:** Mini-Splatting achieves up to 10x model size reduction compared to original 3DGS [S4].
 
 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Significant reduction in memory usage and training time, increased rendering speed, comparable or better rendering quality than vanilla 3DGS [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the hard limit $N_{max}$ and optimization schedule [S2]. Simplification process may introduce artifacts if not properly configured [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Significantly reduced memory footprint and faster training/rendering times while maintaining comparable or superior image quality metrics [S2]. The spatial reorganization approach avoids the artifacts associated with direct pruning [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** The use of fewer, larger Gaussians can lead to noticeable popping artifacts [S4]. The method requires careful tuning of the densification and simplification schedule [S2].
 
 **When to Use:**
-*   Use Mini-Splatting when resource constraints (memory, training time) are a priority and a slight trade-off in rendering quality is acceptable [S2].
-*   Use Mini-Splatting-D when rendering quality is the primary concern and resources are less constrained [S2].
+*   Use Mini-Splatting when memory and compute resources are constrained, and high rendering speed is required [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   Use Mini-Splatting-D when rendering quality is prioritized over memory and speed [S2].
